  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are possible Friday \nnight through Sunday night.\n\nThe center of an 1025 mb high will push south over the Mid West \ntonight. As the high builds south, steady H85 CAA is forecast to \noccur until 12Z Saturday, with values between 0 to -2C. Using a \nblend of guidance, low temperatures Saturday morning are forecast to \nrange from the mid 30s inland to the low to mid 40s across the \ncoastal counties. Northwest winds should range between 5 to 8 mph \nthrough much of tonight, but could decouple during the pre-dawn \nhours. Given temperatures in the mid 30s, light to calm winds, and \nRH in the 90s, areas to patchy frost is expected along and west of \nthe I-95 corridor. The area has been highlighted with a Frost \nAdvisory from 2-8 AM Saturday.\n\nSaturday and Saturday Night: High pressure will be spread along the \nmid and upper MS Valley, with it's periphery stretching into the \nSoutheast U.S. Northerly winds will continue to usher cold air into \nour area. Thickness values and 850 mb temperatures point towards \ncolder temperatures than what some of the MOS has, and we leaned \nthis way with the forecast. Despite full sunshine, high temperatures \nmay struggle to reach the 60 degree mark, which is about 10 degrees \nbelow normal for this time of year. The combination of clear skies \nand calm or very light north to northeasterly winds will lead to \nplenty of radiational cooling. Lows should range from the upper 20s \nin the coldest spots far inland to around freezing closer to the \ncoast, with coastal counties generally in the mid to upper 30s, and \nthe beaches generally in the 40s. Since there is still some \nuncertainty with the temperatures, we maintained the Freeze Watch. \nPlus, this matches our surrounding offices. Though, adjustments may \nbe needed with the areal coverage when it's upgraded to a warning. \nAdditionally, there will be frost within the current watch and \ncloser to the coast (where there are no watches).\n\nSunday and Sunday Night: High pressure along the mid and upper MS \nValley in the morning will slowly shift eastward towards the OH \nValley, with it's periphery continuing to stretch into the Southeast \nU.S. Winds may shift a bit to the northeast during the day, and \ncontinue to usher cold air into our area. Once again, we leaned \ntowards the colder end of guidance. Once again, with plenty of \nsunshine, high temperatures may struggle to reach the 60 degree \nmark. Calm winds and clear skies will lead to plenty of radiational \ncooling the first half of the night. Though, increasing high clouds \nand light winds may limit the full cooling potential the second half \nof the night. As a result, lows should be around freezing very far \ninland, the mid to upper 30s closer to the coast, with locations at \nand near the beaches reaching well into the 40s.\n\nIf we have widespread freezing temperatures Saturday night into \nSunday morning, then that will end our local growing season, and no \nFrost/Freeze products will be needed Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. But if only some areas reach freezing, then the growing \nseason and Frost/Freeze products will continue Sunday night into \nMonday morning. Either way, temperatures will be colder than\nnormal.",
